{"name":"Frog Hollow","hex":"#7da270","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"frog","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of a class of small tailless amphibians of the order Anura that typically hop.","A member of this class that is not a toad."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To hunt or trap frogs.","To use a pronged plater to transfer (cells) to another plate."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/frog"},{"term":"hollow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small valley between mountains.","A sunken area on a surface."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["to make a hole in something; to excavate"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hollow"}],"entries":[{"title":"Frog Hollow, Hartford, Connecticut","blocks":[{"text":"Frog Hollow is one of the neighborhoods of Hartford, Connecticut. It is a predominantly residential area, bounded on the north by Capitol Avenue, the east by Lafayette Street, the south by Madison and Hamilton Streets, and on the west by Interstate 84. The neighborhood was developed between about 1850 and 1930, and still contains a remarkable concentration of residential housing from that period. The entire neighborhood, covering more than 150 acres (61 ha) and including more than 900 buildings, is listed on the National Register of Historic Places."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/HartfordCT_FrogHollow_1.jpg/330px-HartfordCT_FrogHollow_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frog_Hollow%2C_Hartford%2C_Connecticut","wikidata":"Q18648952"},{"title":"Frog","blocks":[{"text":"A frog is any member of a diverse and largely semiaquatic group of short-bodied, tailless amphibian vertebrates composing the order Anura. Frog species with rough skin texture due to wart-like parotoid glands tend to be called toads, but the distinction between frogs and toads is informal and purely cosmetic, and 